---
[contract] Journal Contract v1 (planning)
Promote implicit guarantees to documented IScrivener contract: dense monotonic positions from 1, long-range positions (remove int.MaxValue list ceiling), tail semantics (infinite, non-completing, restartable from position), WaitForAsync scan-from semantics, snapshot-vs-live ReadBackward, thread safety for concurrent writers, completion/disposal behavior, genesis + schema-version entry convention, tombstone convention (undo appends, never rewrites).
> Positions stay dense and monotonic per journal - this is the load-bearing decision that forces a single position authority per journal
> Genesis/schema-version and tombstones adopted from the Geas journal design so the contract serves it natively
> WaitFor semantics split: level-triggered replay becomes explicit; edge-triggered waits become cursor-based (fixes ContractDaemon WaitFor/WaitForFailure replaying history)
---
[contract-tests] Cross-Implementation Contract Suite (notstarted)
One test suite that every IScrivener implementation must pass: InMemory, durable, distributed. Position density, concurrent write ordering, tail-from-position, backward snapshot isolation, capacity, dispose/completion. This is the forcing function the abstraction never had - a second real implementation validated against the first.
-> contract
---
[cursors] Durable Consumer Cursors (planning)
Per-consumer named cursors (pump id, daemon id) persisted alongside the journal. Pumps tail from cursor, not from 0. Retell/replay becomes an explicit operation (new consumer or cursor reset), never an accident of process restart. Daemon-event journal moves to cursor-based waits.
-> contract
> Cursor ownership follows the Tee model: every fan-out consumer has its own cursor; exclusive On groups share one
---
[segment-log] Single-Node Durable Scrivener (notstarted)
Append-only segmented log (JSONL segments + index), fsync policy configurable (per-write for canon journals, batched for telemetry), atomic segment rotation. Write path returns position only after durability per policy. No silent drop on shutdown - flush failures scream.
-> contract
-> contract-tests
> JSONL per Geas journal schema so geas canon --replay reads the raw log without an export step
---
[hydration] Startup Hydration + Crash Recovery (notstarted)
On open: scan segments, verify genesis + schema major, rebuild index, truncate torn tail writes (last-line partial JSON), resume position sequence. Kill -9 during write must recover to a consistent prefix. Recovery is loud: torn writes logged, never silently skipped.
-> segment-log
---
[dedup] Idempotent Pumps (planning)
At-least-once delivery means restart replays entries after the last durable cursor checkpoint. Transmuter effects must be idempotent or deduplicated: target-journal write dedup keyed on (source journal, source position, route id). Decide whether dedup lives in the pump (covenant machinery) or the scrivener write path.
-> cursors
> Prefer pump-level dedup: scriveners stay dumb logs; covenant machinery owns exactly-once-effect
---
[retire-filescrivener] Retire FileScrivener + FlusherDaemon (notstarted)
Current FileScrivener is an in-memory delegator with a write-behind sink: no hydration, no replay, silent buffer drop on shutdown. Superseded by segment-log. Delete per guidelines/coding.md; migrate FileScrivenerConsole toy to the durable scrivener as its smoke test.
-> segment-log
-> hydration
---
[sequencer] Distributed Position Authority (planning)
Dense monotonic positions require a single sequencer per journal. Design the minimal authority: single-writer lease (one node owns append for a journal, others forward), fencing tokens on lease transfer, no multi-master position assignment ever.
-> contract
> Rejected: sparse/vector positions and CRDT merge - Geas source-scoped contradiction is content at the semantic layer, but position order stays total
---
[replication] Replicated Journal Backend (planning)
Replicate the segment log: leader append + follower sync, read-your-writes on leader, followers serve tails with bounded lag. Decision gate first: build minimal replication vs adapt an existing log (NATS JetStream / Kafka / Redis Streams) behind the same contract suite. Adopt if the contract tests pass unmodified; build only if adapters distort the contract.
-> sequencer
-> segment-log
-> contract-tests
> The contract suite is the adopt-vs-build referee - whichever backend passes it without contract exceptions wins
---
[geas-integration] Geas Journal on Durable Scrivener (notstarted)
Geas world folder journal.jsonl backed by the durable scrivener: genesis entry, schema geas/1, tombstone undo, canon --replay as the re-derivability audit running the hydration path. First external consumer; its journal audit benchmark doubles as Coven's durability acceptance test.
-> hydration
-> cursors
